<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gaius Gracchus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 154–121 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Gaius Sempronius Gracchus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gaius Gracchus was a Roman tribune of the plebs in 123 and 122 BCE who continued the reforms of his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>. His laws provided subsidised grain for citizens, gave the equestrians control of the extortion court and founded colonies, including Junonia on the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. When his opponents moved against him in 121 BCE the Senate passed an emergency decree, and he died with thousands of his followers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gaius-gracchus.jpg" alt="Gaius Gracchus" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Baptiste Claude Eugène Guillaume · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eugene_Guillaume_-_the_Gracchi_(cropped)_Gaius.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
